<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Veliky Ustyug</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Veliky Ustyug is a town in Vologda Oblast in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, at the confluence of the Sukhona and Yug rivers, first mentioned in the chronicles in 1207. A frontier outpost of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rostov">Rostov</a> and Vladimir-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suzdal">Suzdal</a> on the route to the northern fur lands, it was captured by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgars">Bulgars</a> around 1218. From the sixteenth to the eighteenth century it was a major trading town on the route between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arkhangelsk">Arkhangelsk</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siberia">Siberia</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/veliky-ustyug.jpg" alt="Veliky Ustyug" width="330" height="220" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Hamerani · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Великий_Устюг,_Успенский_собор2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Veliky Ustyug stands near the confluence of the Sukhona, flowing from the west, and the Yug, flowing from the south. Below the junction the rivers form the Northern Dvina. The name Ustyug means the mouth of the Yug, and by the late 15th century it had become Veliky, or Great, Ustyug. The first recorded settlement was the monastic settlement at Gleden, where the Troitsko-Gledensky Monastery now stands. The historic centre lies on the high left bank of the Sukhona, in an area that was once a posad, a craftsmen's quarter outside the fortress.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>A chronicle first mentions the town in 1207, and the Mikhaylo-Arkhangelsky Monastery was founded in 1212. It belonged to the Vladimir-Suzdal Principality, while neighbouring lands such as Totma were colonised by Novgorod, so it formed the only obstacle to Novgorod's trade with the north along the Sukhona and the Northern Dvina. Clashes between Novgorod and Ustyug became regular, especially in the 14th century. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-duchy-of-moscow">Grand Duchy of Moscow</a> annexed the town in 1328. The Mongol invasion did not directly affect it, and in the second half of the 13th century it grew rapidly with refugees from central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>.</p><p>In 1450 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dmitry-shemyaka">Dmitry Shemyaka</a> took the town during his war with his cousin <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vasily-ii-of-moscow">Vasily II of Moscow</a>, drowned in the Sukhona the citizens who refused to accept him as prince, and held it for two years. Polish troops besieged the town in 1613, during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/time-of-troubles">Time of Troubles</a>, but never took it. At the junction of important trade routes it became a significant commercial and industrial centre in the 16th and 17th centuries. It was also known for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> filigree, birch bark fretwork and niello, for its own Ustyug icon painting and for tiles.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Veliky Ustyug lost its importance as a river port when trade between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> was diverted to the Baltic after the founding of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-petersburg">Saint Petersburg</a> in 1703. It has preserved almost all of its architectural monuments, with churches and cathedrals of the 17th and early 18th centuries, among them the Assumption Cathedral, built between 1619 and 1659. The museum director Nikolay Bekryashev, from 1924 to 1938, persuaded the authorities to hand old churches to the museum rather than demolish them. Many of the town's heritage buildings stand on the left bank of the Sukhona. In Vologda Oblast, it is mostly known for tourism.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Veliky Ustyug” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Raids and counter-raids continued: the Bulgars seized <b>Ustyug</b> around 1218, and in 1220 a Vladimir army sacked the Bulgar town of Oshel.</blockquote><a href="/empire/volga-bulgaria">Volga Bulgaria · Military</a></li></ul></section>
